window.docContents[19].resolve({"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-framework-gained":{"contents":"The demands above were met partly by TorchLean as it stands and partly by changes contributed to\nit. The ledger records each contribution by the demand it serves, with its state on\n2026-11-27.\n\n* Demand* Contribution* State\n* One source, many carriers* exact Cholesky and QR (#2); differentiable exp, log and scalar-affine maps (#5); CPU and proof-side division nodes (#7, #26); the runtime link connected to the Fréchet-derivative model (#25); the TapeM surface reduced to one lemma (#31)* in TorchLean main\n* One source, many carriers* power, the ordered comparisons and selection on device buffers (#52); a piecewise-linear table lookup with its pullback on device buffers, its positions held on the device (#53)* open; merged into combined\n* A bit-exact oracle on every machine* the LibTorch bridge on the host (#43); the identity of a CUDA device (#45); NaN-ignoring fmin and fmax on device buffers, with the same bits on both devices (#50)* open; merged into the fork's combined branch\n* A recording that costs what the model costs* a bounded device block cache (#10); unboxed, concurrent .npy decoding (#29)* in TorchLean main\n* A recording that costs what the model costs* a tape node recorded in linear time (#51)* open; merged into combined\n* Arithmetic intensity* the fused scaled product exponential exp((c·x)·y), bit-identical to its four-operation form (#23)* in TorchLean main\n* Arithmetic intensity* conversions between scalars and indices in a certified kernel (#44, answering issue #42); binary64 arithmetic on binary32 buffers (#47); a conditional directly in an else branch certified (#46)* open; merged into combined\n\nTwo contributions on the floating-point side stand closed unmerged, the theory they extended\nhaving moved to FloatLib: Sterbenz's lemma for formats with an underflow floor\n(#16), which FloatLib now proves, and an\nexecutable unit in the last place with an absorption test\n(#17), whose absorption test TorchLean carries\nand whose executable ULP the calculus defines itself, in its numerical-adequacy layer.\n\nFive further needs were met by TorchLean's maintainers in their own way, and the corresponding\ncontributions stand closed in favor of that work: an asserted leak bound for long eager loops\n(#8,\n#15), release of tape temporaries\n(#9), a glibc link failure in CUDA builds\n(#18), and the choice of CUDA target\narchitecture (#27). That independent\nconvergence is the plainest evidence that these needs are the framework's, not one model's.\n\nThree of the contributions follow from the table fetch alone, and they show how a metrological\nchoice becomes a framework capability. A lookup is a data-dependent read, so the kernel language\nneeds an index computed from a coordinate (⌊u⌋) and the weight computed back from the index\n(u − ⌊u⌋): #44. Linear interpolation in binary32 has no formula free of all three of overflow,\nan inexact flat segment and cancellation, so the kernel computes in binary64 on binary32 buffers\nand rounds once: #47. The device program then mirrors the host reference's chain of\nconditionals instead of nesting every conditional in a then branch: #46.\n\nRequiring the CPU and device runs to agree bit for bit makes them a differential test of the\nframework and of the libraries under it, and the test found three defects there. ATen's CPU\nlog1p returns 0 at the least subnormal of binary32 and of binary64. ATen's CPU fmax\nreturns the first of a pair of zeros where CUDA orders them. __float2ull_rz of a NaN is not\n0 on an SM 8.6 device. The first is repaired in #43, the second avoided in #50, and the third\nguarded in #44's emitted code.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","header":"19.3. What the framework gained","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework--What-the-framework-gained"},"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Lesson":{"contents":"1. Open the box and represent configurations. Each body's upstream harmonic oscillator\nsupplies its wall-spring parameters; the coupling spring supplies stiffness, and each spring\nhas a signed extension. Two CoupledPair .P configurations admit a typed difference of\ntheir kC extensions:\n\n\n\nMass is now indexed by a body part and stiffness by a spring part. The oscillator-indexed\nL1 ω² was the frequency of an isolated oscillator; it is not the normal-mode frequency\nof this coupled pair.\n\n2. Assemble the pair's mass. The two body masses are parts of Composite.whole; rfl\nchecks that the total reads the upstream oscillator fields:\n\n\n\n3. Refuse whole plus part, and one pair plus another. Total mass is meaningful, but its\nwhole is not body A. A second pair is another system even when all parameters and part\nnames match, because PairId is nominal:\n\n\n\n4. Refuse to assemble angular frequency. The pair's license table has no entry for it:\n\n\n\n5. Give the normal mode its whole. A dedicated angular-frequency kind at pairS is\ndistinct from one dedicated to a body or a spring:\n\n\n\n6. Assemble parallel stiffness. With B clamped, A is held by kA and kC in parallel:\n\n\n\n7. Refuse series stiffness. For kA and kB chained end to end, no additive series license\nis registered:\n\n\n\n8. Check the licenses against measurements. The measurements use HCP's own Spring.\nPropertyValue.numeral is fixed at Int, so the exhibited kA–kB series chain is exact\n(6 ∥ 3 = 2); these integer numerals do not model longer chains:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nThe measurements verify the curation: parallel stiffness is extensive, while series\nstiffness is whole-proper. Measurement carries an Int numeral, so the exhibited\nkA–kB series value is exact (6 ∥ 3 = 2); these integer numerals do not model longer chains.\nRegistering the series sum would produce a magnitude that disagrees with its measurement.\n\nThe System-context card section of this chapter lists the bodies, all three springs,\nthe fixed walls, and the line of motion. The Lean model's nominal PartOf π index keeps\nparts of differently identified pairs distinct even when their parameters match, while\nbody mass, spring stiffness, and the pair's normal-mode frequency stay on their respective\nbearers. The on-axis, rigid, homogeneous, point-like body assumption returns at L3.\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty\u0009Onboarding: Human Context Protocols\u0009L2 — The pair is a system too","header":"5.3.7. Lesson","id":"/#A-Property-Kind-Calculus-for-Metrology___-proving-quantity-and-unit-laws-in-Lean___-from-ISO___IEC-80000-to-measurement-uncertainty--Onboarding___-Human-Context-Protocols--L2-___-The-pair-is-a-system-too--Lesson"}});